---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const questions = [
  { question: 'Does El Niño mean no snow in Seattle or Portland?', answer: 'No. A warmer seasonal tendency does not rule out a short cold spell or a lowland snowstorm. City snowfall depends on the air mass, storm track and timing of precipitation; a regional ENSO label cannot predict those details.' },
  { question: 'Does La Niña guarantee a big Cascade snowpack?', answer: 'No. Cooler conditions can support snow accumulation, but precipitation, snow levels and later melt still determine the snowpack. The USDA Northwest Climate Hub notes that the precipitation relationship is less consistent than the temperature relationship.' },
  { question: 'Can a warmer winter still contain a major snowstorm?', answer: 'Yes. A three-month average can be warmer than normal while a short period is cold and snowy. A seasonal temperature category does not describe every day in that season.' },
];
---
<Layout title="El Niño vs La Niña: Pacific Northwest Winter & Snowpack" active="winter" description="What ENSO means for Washington, Oregon and Idaho winters: temperature and precipitation tendencies, Cascade snowpack, Seattle and Portland snow, and forecast limits.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage lifecycle-page">
    <div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">WASHINGTON · OREGON · IDAHO · REVIEWED NOVEMBER 12, 2026</span><h1>Pacific Northwest<br /><span>winter &amp; snowpack.</span></h1><p>El Niño often favors warmer winters; La Niña often favors cooler ones. Neither phase tells you how much snow will fall at your home or ski area.</p></div></div>
    <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/images/guides/northwest-snow-usfs.jpg" alt="A skier descending a snow-covered mountain beside a forested slope near Yellow Pine, Idaho" width="1400" height="877" /><figcaption>Mountain snow near Yellow Pine, Idaho. USDA Forest Service photo by Todd Leeds, reproduced from the <a href="https://www.climatehubs.usda.gov/hubs/northwest/topic/la-nina-northwest-what-can-we-expect">USDA Northwest Climate Hub</a>. Location photograph; no current snow-condition information is implied.</figcaption></figure>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Compare the phases without promising the outcome.</h2><p class="body-copy">The USDA Northwest Climate Hub describes fall and winter in Washington, Oregon and Idaho as usually warmer and drier during El Niño. During La Niña, winter is usually cooler and can be wetter; the precipitation association is less consistent than the temperature association. These are historical tendencies, strongest in winter, rather than local numerical forecasts.</p><p class="body-copy">A warmer winter can shift some precipitation from snow to rain. Cooler conditions can help mountain snow accumulate, but a cold season with little precipitation need not build a large snowpack. Do not apply these three-state relationships unchanged to Alaska, which has different regional responses.</p><p class="body-copy">For winter 2026–27, read the <a href="/winter-outlook/">saved October 15 CPC outlook</a> alongside the <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/">latest official outlook</a>. This evergreen guide does not assign new probabilities to that winter.</p></section>
    <section class="regional-section"><h2>Three different snow questions</h2><div class="regional-grid">
      <article><h3>Seattle and Portland snow</h3><p>Lowland snow needs sufficiently cold air and precipitation at the same time. A seasonal warm or cool tendency cannot predict a city's snow date, total or road conditions.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Cascades and Idaho mountains</h3><p>Elevation and the freezing level change where precipitation falls as rain or snow. Base and summit conditions may differ in the same storm. Check local snow-level and wind forecasts before a trip.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Spring water supply</h3><p>Snow water equivalent measures water stored in snow. Depth measures thickness. Later rain, warmth and melting affect how much water remains and when it reaches rivers.</p></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Does less snow mean summer drought is certain?</h2><p class="body-copy">No. Reduced snow storage can lower later runoff and summer water availability, a pathway described by USDA for the region. It is not a complete drought forecast. Actual snowpack, subsequent precipitation, melt timing, soil moisture and water storage all matter. A wet storm or flood can also occur during an otherwise dry season.</p><p class="body-copy">Track observations through winter instead of carrying the ENSO label forward as a fixed summer outcome. For a ski trip, start with <a href="https://www.weather.gov/">local NWS forecasts and warnings</a>; for seasonal context, compare the valid dates and categories in CPC's outlook.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="snow-drought-heading"><h2 id="snow-drought-heading">How can a wet winter still have snow drought?</h2><p class="body-copy">Snow drought means unusually low snowpack for the time of year. <a href="https://www.drought.gov/topics/snow-drought">Drought.gov distinguishes two pathways</a>: dry snow drought comes from below-normal cold-season precipitation; warm snow drought can occur despite near-normal precipitation when more falls as rain or snow melts early. Warm and dry conditions can also occur together. An ENSO phase alone cannot diagnose which pathway is occurring in a basin.</p><p class="body-copy">Check snow water equivalent alongside precipitation and temperature observations. A low-elevation station and a high mountain basin can tell different stories. Before comparing two “percent of normal” values, match their dates, locations, metric and reference statistic. See <a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/#swe-percent-heading">how to read SWE percentages</a>, including why a same-day median is different from a seasonal peak.</p><p class="body-copy">These are definitions, reviewed November 12, 2026, not a declaration of current Northwest snow drought. Read the valid date on any observation map and use <a href="/winter-outlook/#forecast-horizon-heading">the forecast window that matches your decision</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="northwest-faq"><h2 id="northwest-faq">Northwest winter questions</h2>{questions.map(item => <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>{item.question}</h3><p class="body-copy">{item.answer}</p></div>)}</section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Sources and related guides</h2><p class="body-copy">Reviewed November 12, 2026: USDA Northwest Climate Hub, <a href="https://www.climatehubs.usda.gov/hubs/northwest/topic/el-nino-northwest-what-can-we-expect">El Niño in the Northwest</a> and <a href="https://www.climatehubs.usda.gov/hubs/northwest/topic/la-nina-northwest-what-can-we-expect">La Niña in the Northwest</a>. The La Niña article includes a 2024–25 outlook; only its general mechanisms and regional relationships are used here. Neither source's embedded outlook probabilities are republished as current.</p><p class="body-copy">Compare <a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/">California rain versus Sierra snow</a>, <a href="/forecast/enso-neutral/">ENSO-neutral conditions</a> and the <a href="/impacts/">regional impact guides</a>.</p></section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: questions.map(item => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.answer } })) }).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')} />
